Fork of buildroot set up to cross compile Phantom Satellite (and probably other UXP based browsers, as long as they can build with python 3)

Build instructions:

Clone this repo: 
git clone https://github.com/DCFUKSURMOM/ps-buildroot

CD into the ps-buildroot directory:
cd ps-buildroot

Chose the config file:
make PS-xxxx_defconfig (replace xxxx with the architecture you want)

Start the build:
make -jX (replace X with the number of CPU cores you want to use)

The build will take a while, buildroot isnt just compiling the browser, but any dependencies the browser needs to run.

Once the browser finishes building, it will be packaged into a portable tarball and copied to the images directory (output/images)

Working configs:
i586-nosse (PS-PMMX_defconfig), special config that disables SSE1 and SSE2 (and enables MMX), requires a Pentium MMX or newer
i686-nosse2 (PS-i686-nosse2_defconfig), special config that disables SSE2 (and enables SSE1), requires a Pentium 3 or newer
i686 (PS-i686_defconfig), requires a Pentium 4 or newer
x86_64 (PS-x64_defconfig), should work on any x86_64 CPU
aarch64/ARM64 (PS-ARM64_defconfig), should work on any aarch64/ARM64 CPU
PowerPC (PS-PPC_defconfig), requires a PPC 604e or newer to run, but is optimized for PPC 750 or newer
PowerPC64 (PS-PPC64_defconfig), should work on any 64 bit PowerPC CPU

Not Working:
armv7 (no defconfig), fails to build, minimum requirements unknown.

Other architectures are currently untested.

If your host machine is the same architecture as the architecture you want to compile, it will be faster to compile natively, this tool is intended for cross compiling.

Original buildroot readme below:

Buildroot is a simple, efficient and easy-to-use tool to generate embedded
Linux systems through cross-compilation.

The documentation can be found in docs/manual. You can generate a text
document with 'make manual-text' and read output/docs/manual/manual.text.
Online documentation can be found at https://buildroot.org/docs.html

To build and use the buildroot stuff, do the following:

1) run 'make menuconfig'
2) select the target architecture and the packages you wish to compile
3) run 'make'
4) wait while it compiles
5) find the kernel, bootloader, root filesystem, etc. in output/images

You do not need to be root to build or run buildroot.  Have fun!

Buildroot comes with a basic configuration for a number of boards. Run
'make list-defconfigs' to view the list of provided configurations.

Please feed suggestions, bug reports, insults, and bribes back to the
buildroot mailing list: buildroot@buildroot.org
You can also find us on #buildroot on OFTC IRC.

If you would like to contribute patches, please read
https://buildroot.org/manual.html#submitting-patches
